1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
It's explosive and corrosive.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:06,000
That's some strong .

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Yet it's the most widely used chemical in the world.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
If controlled, it will polish metal.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
But if unleashed, it will dissolve it in seconds.

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Once it gets on your skin, you're burnt.

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
From the depths of the Earth's core to the depths of your stomach,

8
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
this chemical is a cut above the rest.

9
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Now, Acid on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Each year, the United States manufactures approximately 100 million tons of acid.

11
00:00:50,001 --> 00:00:50,701
of acid.

12
00:00:50,702 --> 00:00:57,620
That's equal to a daily production of two pounds per person for an entire year.

13
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:03,641
What's all that acid used for? Just about everything.

14
00:01:03,642 --> 00:01:05,920
Many of the things that you touch

15
00:01:05,921 --> 00:01:11,169
and see every day have used acid in their manufacture.

16
00:01:11,170 --> 00:01:14,960
Plastics, films, textiles, fertilizers.

17
00:01:14,961 --> 00:01:20,900
it's hard to imagine a thing that hadn't had an acid used in its production at some point.

18
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:25,280
Yet despite all the products acid helps to create,

19
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:30,520
this essential chemical of industry is equally prized because it can destroy.

20
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:37,580
One of the most widespread and dangerous acids is nitric acid.

21
00:01:39,260 --> 00:01:42,080
The U.S. produces 8 million tons a year,

22
00:01:42,081 --> 00:01:48,980
And it's the primary catalyst in the 3 million tons of explosives detonated annually in North America.

23
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:55,260
Mainly in the form of ammonium nitrate fuel oil, or ANFO.

24
00:01:55,820 --> 00:02:02,120
Nitric acid in its concentrated form is one of the most powerful oxidizing acids that there is.

25
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:06,440
Nitric acid is the engine that powers the explosion.

26
00:02:06,441 --> 00:02:13,780
When nitric acid reacts with a substance like glycerin, its nitrogen oxide atoms attach to

27
00:02:13,781 --> 00:02:18,120
the newly formed material, nitroglycerin.

28
00:02:18,121 --> 00:02:23,260
During detonation, the nitrate molecules steal electrons, causing a rapid decomposition

29
00:02:23,261 --> 00:02:30,880
that generates gas and heat.

30
00:02:30,881 --> 00:02:40,660
ANFO is the leading explosive used in civil construction.

31
00:02:40,661 --> 00:02:48,800
For military explosives, it's RDX.

32
00:02:48,801 --> 00:02:55,320
RDX detonates with more than twice the explosive velocity of ANFO.

33
00:02:55,321 --> 00:02:59,920
It's the key ingredient in over five dozen explosive formulations used by the U.S.

34
00:02:59,921 --> 00:03:00,621
Armed

35
00:03:00,622 --> 00:03:01,322
forces.

36
00:03:01,323 --> 00:03:08,140
The Holston Army Ammunition Plant in Kingsport, Tennessee, is the largest supplier

37
00:03:08,141 --> 00:03:11,388
of high-performance explosives for the military.

38
00:03:11,389 --> 00:03:14,500
Keeping pace with demand requires vast amounts

39
00:03:14,501 --> 00:03:15,758
of nitric acid.

40
00:03:15,759 --> 00:03:21,880
Armored in protective clothing, workers prepare to offload 180,000 pounds

41
00:03:21,881 --> 00:03:27,140
of nitric acid, which will be used to synthesize RDX.

42
00:03:27,141 --> 00:03:31,940
The nitric acid that we're offloading is what we consider concentrated nitric acid.

43
00:03:31,941 --> 00:03:38,020
It's 98% nitric acid with a small amount of water.

44
00:03:38,021 --> 00:03:42,020
Mechanic Jackie Emmert has worked around this hazardous chemical for over two decades.

45
00:03:42,021 --> 00:03:45,400
You better not take it for granted because if you do, you'll pay for it.

46
00:03:45,401 --> 00:03:48,420
You don't get a second chance.

47
00:03:48,421 --> 00:03:52,560
Once it gets on your skin, you're burnt.

48
00:03:52,561 --> 00:03:56,740
It eats down into your skin until they get something on it to stop it.

49
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:58,520
It'll probably just burn onto the bone.

50
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:05,260
Once the nitric acid is safely stored away,

51
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:09,640
it will serve as a key component in manufacturing RDX.

52
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:15,860
Inside an 8-foot-tall nitration tank,

53
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:20,000
nitric acid is mixed with hexamine and several additional chemicals.

54
00:04:20,001 --> 00:04:26,220
The mix is heated to 150 degrees Fahrenheit and agitated at 140 RPMs.

55
00:04:26,221 --> 00:04:32,000
In 40 minutes, the chemical cocktail crystallizes into RDX.

56
00:04:32,001 --> 00:04:41,000
This volatile ingredient, born from acid, will now serve as the engine for 70 different types of explosives.

57
00:04:41,001 --> 00:04:51,000
Mixing RDX with Molten TNT produces Composition B4, an explosive used to detonate minefields.

58
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:03,000
We have RDX and Molten TNT that go into a mixed kettle, just like you would mix a cake batter,

59
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
and then it's dropped to a pellet pot and onto a stainless steel belt.

60
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,760
The explosive cocktail exits the pellet pot at 226 degrees Fahrenheit and travels through

61
00:05:17,761 --> 00:05:21,120
cold water, which solidifies the material.

62
00:05:21,121 --> 00:05:29,720
It then falls into a collection bin, looking more like peanut brittle minus the peanuts

63
00:05:29,721 --> 00:05:31,960
than a deadly explosive.

64
00:05:31,961 --> 00:05:36,440
Handling it from here is not for the faint of heart.

65
00:05:36,441 --> 00:05:43,480
It can be impact sensitive, but that would mean taking a hammer and putting it on a rock and hitting it with a hammer.

66
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:47,820
That would be dangerous, but the way we handle it, it is a very stable product.

67
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:59,080
In addition to Composition B4, Holsten also produces C4, a general purpose explosive strong enough to blast through a steel door.

68
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:03,860
What we're going to be demonstrating today is about 70 grams of our product.

69
00:06:03,861 --> 00:06:11,720
the c4 is packed inside a 16 ounce cup and placed on top of a one-quarter inch thick steel plate

70
00:06:11,721 --> 00:06:25,160
and this is the demonstration with a quarter inch boilerplate steel after 70 grams of material a

71
00:06:25,161 --> 00:06:31,040
closer look reveals that the force of the explosion imprinted the number 16 from the cup onto the

72
00:06:31,041 --> 00:06:32,109
steel plate.

73
00:06:32,110 --> 00:06:39,500
Despite the fact that nitric acid is a central ingredient in nearly every explosive

74
00:06:39,501 --> 00:06:44,020
cocktail, most of it goes toward the production of ammonium nitrate fertilizer.

75
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:52,460
The U.S. makes 2.5 million tons every year, but even in fertilizer,

76
00:06:52,980 --> 00:06:56,470
nitric acid's explosive potential never stays dormant.

77
00:06:56,471 --> 00:06:59,120
Ammonium nitrate is a popular fertilizer,

78
00:06:59,121 --> 00:07:02,540
but it can also be used as an oxidizer for explosives.

79
00:07:03,320 --> 00:07:07,580
In that case, it has to be mixed with a fuel, in this case, powdered zinc.

80
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:12,360
Just a little bit of water will get it started.

81
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:18,200
A simple drop of water starts a chemical reaction that ignites the mixture.

82
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:28,760
In 1947, nitric acid's volatility triggered disaster.

83
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:33,360
It happened in Texas City, Texas.

84
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:41,760
The port city was nearly leveled when a freighter packed with ammonium nitrate fertilizer exploded.

85
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:47,800
581 people were killed, and another 5,000 were injured.

86
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:58,420
The Texas City disaster is considered the worst industrial accident in the history of the United States.

87
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:05,620
We now return to acid on Modern Marvels.

88
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:09,960
Although nitric acid is a powerful chemical,

89
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:12,660
there's another acid that's even stronger,

90
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:15,160
sulfuric acid.

91
00:08:16,580 --> 00:08:19,460
Sulfuric acid is classified as a strong acid

92
00:08:19,461 --> 00:08:23,760
because it contains a high concentration of hydrogen ions.

93
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:28,520
The pH scale measures the strength of an acid.

94
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:32,340
Water, which is a neutral liquid, has a pH of 7.

95
00:08:33,300 --> 00:08:36,300
A pH with a number greater than 7 is a base,

96
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:40,020
while a pH with a number less than 7 is an acid.

97
00:08:40,940 --> 00:08:46,240
Each number less than neutral contains 10 times the hydrogen ions of the next greater number.

98
00:08:47,340 --> 00:08:50,760
Therefore, concentrated sulfuric acid, which has a pH of 1,

99
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:55,740
is 100,000 times more acidic than saliva, which has a pH of 6.

100
00:08:55,741 --> 00:09:05,100
As with all strong acids, when sulfuric acid is added to water or a base, its hydrogen ions break off, generating heat.

101
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:12,120
The heat from the dissociation is being absorbed by a relatively small amount of water,

102
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:19,640
and the solution is getting so hot that it actually melts the plastic cup as well as the dropper.

103
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:24,120
And this is why you don't want to add water to acid.

104
00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:32,240
Besides being a strong acid, sulfuric acid is also highly corrosive to most metals, including aluminum.

105
00:09:34,940 --> 00:09:39,820
I've got aluminum foil here and sulfuric acid.

106
00:09:39,821 --> 00:09:53,821
The fog that's coming off here is actually steam that's being generated as the reaction

107
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:01,660
proceeds.

108
00:10:01,661 --> 00:10:09,260
None of the aluminum foil remains.

109
00:10:09,261 --> 00:10:14,400
Sulfuric acid is also a powerful dehydrator capable of drawing the moisture out of substances

110
00:10:14,401 --> 00:10:16,260
such as sugar.

111
00:10:16,261 --> 00:10:22,920
What we're seeing here after the sugar has lost its water all that's left is carbon and

112
00:10:22,921 --> 00:10:27,840
it rises out of the beaker as a solid ash.

113
00:10:27,841 --> 00:10:32,460
If a single drop of sulfuric acid gets on your skin it will treat you just like it does

114
00:10:32,461 --> 00:10:38,700
sugar by absorbing the skin's moisture which in turn generates heat. Not so sweet.

115
00:10:44,380 --> 00:10:49,420
In Mulberry, Florida, the Mosaic Company produces sulfuric acid on a massive scale.

116
00:10:50,620 --> 00:10:53,660
If a drop is dangerous, well, you do the math.

117
00:10:54,540 --> 00:10:58,092
We produce 35,000 tons a day of sulfuric acid.

118
00:10:58,093 --> 00:11:01,180
If we were to put that into perspective,

119
00:11:01,181 --> 00:11:03,680
An average automobile weighs about two tons.

120
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,760
That would be like 17,000 automobiles parked in a parking lot every day.

121
00:11:08,660 --> 00:11:11,100
But sulfuric acid is used in the petroleum industry,

122
00:11:11,980 --> 00:11:12,800
leaching industry,

123
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:15,080
pulp and paper industry.

124
00:11:15,980 --> 00:11:19,420
It is used to clean the large vats in beer production

125
00:11:19,421 --> 00:11:22,380
so that each batch of beer tastes the same as it did previous.

126
00:11:26,060 --> 00:11:28,520
Regardless of sulfuric acid's end use,

127
00:11:29,080 --> 00:11:30,520
it begins with, well,

128
00:11:30,521 --> 00:11:38,720
sulfur of course at mosaic it arrives by rail car sulfur is a solid state at

129
00:11:38,721 --> 00:11:43,540
ambient temperatures we have to heat it up to approximately 270 degrees

130
00:11:43,541 --> 00:11:50,740
Fahrenheit transforming molten sulfur into sulfuric acid starts by spraying

131
00:11:50,741 --> 00:11:58,960
it through a sulfur gun that expels it into a furnace at 2055 degrees Fahrenheit

132
00:11:58,961 --> 00:12:03,460
the sulfur combusts with oxygen to form sulfur dioxide gas.

133
00:12:04,740 --> 00:12:07,880
From there, the sulfur dioxide travels to a converter.

134
00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:12,560
Combined with oxygen, it passes through a catalyst

135
00:12:12,561 --> 00:12:15,540
that gradually converts it into sulfur trioxide.

136
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:19,800
The sulfur trioxide then enters an absorption tower

137
00:12:19,801 --> 00:12:23,020
where it's combined with water in a sulfuric acid solution,

138
00:12:23,620 --> 00:12:25,320
creating additional sulfuric acid.

139
00:12:25,321 --> 00:12:32,420
Since the reaction of water and sulfuric acid produces heat, the tower is constantly monitored

140
00:12:32,421 --> 00:12:36,700
and controlled.

141
00:12:36,701 --> 00:12:41,800
Mosaic circulates 40,000 gallons of cold water per minute to keep the tower operating at

142
00:12:41,801 --> 00:12:45,380
a safe and efficient temperature.

143
00:12:45,381 --> 00:12:51,500
We have a cooling system here behind me that's referred to as a cooling tower and it allows

144
00:12:51,501 --> 00:12:56,360
water to evaporate and cool the water which is then recirculated back to the

145
00:12:56,361 --> 00:13:04,640
plant and used to cool the acid. After the sulfuric acid is produced, operators

146
00:13:04,641 --> 00:13:09,540
wearing acid-resistant suits discharge it into trucks.

147
00:13:10,660 --> 00:13:16,900
The acid is then distributed to various satellite plants to make fertilizer.

148
00:13:16,901 --> 00:13:23,900
Although a powerful corrosive, at 98.5% concentration,

149
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:28,220
sulfuric acid is powerless against the stainless steel enclosure of the truck.

150
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:33,760
Ever wonder why your eyes water and burn around cut onions?

151
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:38,200
Onions are high in sulfur and emit a gas that reacts with the moisture in your eyes,

152
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:40,340
forming a solution of sulfuric acid.

153
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:42,480
Tears help to dilute the acid.

154
00:13:43,220 --> 00:13:45,280
Acid will return on Modern Marvels.

155
00:13:46,900 --> 00:13:50,060
We now return to Acid on Modern Marvels.

156
00:13:54,680 --> 00:13:58,680
Well, an acid's ability to dissolve metal is a simple rule of science.

157
00:13:59,340 --> 00:14:02,880
In Hollywood, flesh-eating acid is a product of science fiction.

158
00:14:04,440 --> 00:14:07,600
So how long does it take for acid to dissolve a body?

159
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:15,300
Everyone wonders what it would be like to fall into a vat of acid.

160
00:14:15,301 --> 00:14:22,800
What I have here is 37% hydrochloric acid, which is as concentrated as hydrochloric acid gets.

161
00:14:23,320 --> 00:14:27,940
As a stand-in for the body, I've got a hot dog and a chicken bone.

162
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:34,040
After six hours, we're starting to see some action.

163
00:14:34,840 --> 00:14:44,260
The bone is starting to get rather floppy, and the hot dog has fallen to pieces.

164
00:14:45,300 --> 00:14:51,780
After nine hours, the hot dog is nowhere to be found,

165
00:14:51,781 --> 00:14:56,520
and the bone is in pretty sad shape.

166
00:14:57,740 --> 00:15:00,680
So after nine hours in hydrochloric acid,

167
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:03,840
your body is going to be completely disintegrated.

168
00:15:04,200 --> 00:15:07,900
Fortunately for you, the fumes would have killed you long before that.

169
00:15:12,100 --> 00:15:15,020
Hydrochloric acid, albeit a dilute concentration,

170
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:18,120
is the same acid the stomach makes to dissolve food.

171
00:15:22,680 --> 00:15:27,040
It's also the acid that's a key step in making the ubiquitous substance gelatin,

172
00:15:27,780 --> 00:15:30,500
a tasteless protein that puts the gel in Jell-O.

173
00:15:32,960 --> 00:15:36,720
The Eastman Gelatin Corporation, located in Peabody, Massachusetts,

174
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:39,420
has been making gelatin for nearly a century.

175
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:44,540
Getting to the bare bones of the process starts with just that.

176
00:15:45,300 --> 00:15:48,300
Bones.

177
00:15:48,301 --> 00:15:55,300
Each of the rail cars that comes into the plant contains about 200,000 pounds of bone.

178
00:15:55,301 --> 00:15:57,300
The bone is being unloaded from a rail car.

179
00:15:57,301 --> 00:16:01,300
It goes through a sifter, which has some screens of different sizes,

180
00:16:01,301 --> 00:16:04,300
and we move the very fine material we call bone meal.

181
00:16:04,301 --> 00:16:07,300
The larger pieces of bone go into a storage bin.

182
00:16:07,301 --> 00:16:16,760
We use hydrochloric acid because it reacts very effectively with the bone.

183
00:16:16,761 --> 00:16:20,440
The hydrochloric acid is removing the minerals from the bone.

184
00:16:20,441 --> 00:16:24,340
The minerals are essentially the concrete in the reinforced concrete.

185
00:16:24,341 --> 00:16:28,620
And we're reacting with that, removing the minerals, leaving behind the rebar.

186
00:16:28,621 --> 00:16:36,920
The rebar is the protein which we're going to make the gelatin out of.

187
00:16:36,921 --> 00:16:43,120
It's a variable process that requires some old school methods.

188
00:16:43,121 --> 00:16:47,880
A stick test helps determine whether the bone is ready for the next step.

189
00:16:47,881 --> 00:16:50,660
The bone that has been demineralized is not as dense.

190
00:16:50,661 --> 00:16:54,380
The stick can push its way down through it.

191
00:16:54,381 --> 00:17:00,440
So that's our way of telling how much of the bone has been demineralized.

192
00:17:00,441 --> 00:17:06,760
After the acid demineralizes the bone, the bone is transferred to a lime bath.

193
00:17:06,761 --> 00:17:12,900
Here, lime finishes the job of breaking down the collagen proteins that have been exposed by the hydrochloric acid.

194
00:17:15,360 --> 00:17:19,580
The bone is then washed and pumped into an extraction vat for gelatin removal.

195
00:17:24,240 --> 00:17:27,660
What I'm holding my hand here is essentially what I call the rebar.

196
00:17:27,980 --> 00:17:32,020
So we've removed the minerals to get the concrete out of the way, and what we're left with is just the protein.

197
00:17:32,021 --> 00:17:43,780
The gelatin is extracted in a hot water solution and pumped through an extruder, falling onto

198
00:17:43,781 --> 00:17:46,060
a conveyor looking like wet spaghetti noodles.

199
00:17:46,061 --> 00:17:51,540
What I'm holding in my hand here is 30% gelatin and 70% water.

200
00:17:51,541 --> 00:17:58,140
As soon as you put it in your mouth, it would melt and it would have no flavor at all.

201
00:17:58,141 --> 00:18:06,140
The gelatin is then cooked until it's bone dry, cut into granules, boxed and shipped.

202
00:18:06,141 --> 00:18:13,960
From this point on, it can be used in a variety of different products, including film emulsions,

203
00:18:13,961 --> 00:18:19,300
pharmaceuticals, golf balls, and foods.

204
00:18:19,301 --> 00:18:23,420
Bon Appetit.

205
00:18:23,421 --> 00:18:29,560
While gelatin's lack of flavor makes it a valued food additive, acid's sour flavor makes

206
00:18:29,561 --> 00:18:33,140
it a prized food ingredient.

207
00:18:33,141 --> 00:18:37,820
Before me I have a range of acidic materials that we consume every day.

208
00:18:37,821 --> 00:18:43,220
Oranges, which contain citric acid, will have a pH of about 3.8.

209
00:18:43,221 --> 00:18:48,860
Ginger ale, which also contains citric acid, has a pH of about 3.

210
00:18:48,861 --> 00:18:53,580
A cola contains phosphoric acid, and it's going to be about 2.6.

211
00:18:54,460 --> 00:19:01,120
And finally, we have red wine vinegar, which is the most acidic of these materials.

212
00:19:01,121 --> 00:19:09,800
And as with all acids, vinegar has a wonderful sour taste.

213
00:19:12,700 --> 00:19:15,380
Vinegar is hardly deemed a refreshing beverage,

214
00:19:15,980 --> 00:19:19,100
but Americans consume more than 6 million tons of it a year.

215
00:19:20,740 --> 00:19:25,620
One of the oldest and largest commercial manufacturers of vinegar is Heinz.

216
00:19:27,980 --> 00:19:30,400
Here at the Heinz plant in Holland, Michigan,

217
00:19:31,060 --> 00:19:33,320
making vinegar starts with a whole lot of spirits.

218
00:19:33,640 --> 00:19:39,280
We get grain source alcohol made only from grain corn,

219
00:19:39,700 --> 00:19:42,780
shipped to us in 30,000-gallon rail cars,

220
00:19:42,980 --> 00:19:45,240
and it comes in at 190 proof.

221
00:19:45,380 --> 00:19:47,260
which is 95% alcohol.

222
00:19:48,940 --> 00:19:51,480
The alcohol is offloaded into a storage tank

223
00:19:51,481 --> 00:19:56,140
and then pumped into a series of 18,000-gallon mash tanks

224
00:19:56,141 --> 00:19:57,800
where it's added to water,

225
00:19:58,440 --> 00:20:00,940
producing a 13.5% concentration.

226
00:20:06,120 --> 00:20:09,080
The next step is adding a mix of nutrients to the mash

227
00:20:09,081 --> 00:20:11,220
that will help promote the growth of a little friend

228
00:20:11,221 --> 00:20:13,160
that plays a big part in making vinegar,

229
00:20:14,060 --> 00:20:14,800
acetobacter.

230
00:20:15,380 --> 00:20:25,640
Acetobacter is a microorganism and the metabolism and growth of that organism is what facilitates

231
00:20:25,641 --> 00:20:30,540
the oxidation of alcohol to acetic acid.

232
00:20:30,541 --> 00:20:39,060
